UPDATE blog_posts SET localizations = jsonb_set(localizations, ARRAY['contentZhHant'], to_jsonb('## [Vancouver](/en/areas/vancouver/) Renovation Guide for New Immigrants: From Buying to Moving In (2026)

This guide is written for newcomers to Vancouver who have recently purchased a home and are planning renovations. It covers the entire process from understanding the Canadian renovation industry to hiring a contractor and navigating permits.

### Understanding the BC Renovation Industry

The renovation industry in British Columbia is different from many other countries. Key things to know:

**Contractor licensing:** BC does not currently require a general contractor licence for residential renovation, but reputable contractors carry business licences, WorkSafe BC coverage, and general liability insurance. Always verify these before hiring.

**Building permits:** Major renovation work (structural changes, plumbing, electrical, adding a suite) requires a building permit from your local municipality. Permit requirements vary by city — Vancouver, [Burnaby](/en/areas/burnaby/), [Richmond](/en/areas/richmond/), [Surrey](/en/areas/surrey/), and other cities each have their own rules and fee schedules.

**Building code:** All renovation work must comply with the BC Building Code, which sets standards for structural safety, fire protection, energy efficiency, plumbing, and electrical systems.

### Step-by-Step Renovation Process for New Homeowners

**1. Home inspection before purchase:** Before you buy, hire a licensed home inspector to identify existing issues — electrical problems, plumbing leaks, roof condition, foundation cracks, asbestos, mold. This report helps you budget for necessary repairs and renovations.

**2. Priority planning:** Not everything needs to be renovated at once. Prioritize:
- Safety issues first (electrical, plumbing, structural)
- Functional improvements (kitchen, bathrooms)
- Cosmetic updates last (paint, flooring, fixtures)

**3. Budgeting:** Set aside 10–20% of your renovation budget as contingency for unexpected issues. Older homes (pre-1980) often reveal hidden problems once walls are opened — asbestos, knob-and-tube wiring, water damage, or outdated framing.

**4. Getting quotes:** Get 3+ written quotes from different contractors. Compare them line by line, not just the total. The cheapest quote is rarely the best value — look for detailed, transparent pricing.

**5. Understanding contracts:** A proper contract protects both you and the contractor. It should include detailed scope, materials specification, timeline, payment schedule, warranty terms, and change order process. Never pay 100% upfront — a typical payment schedule is 10–30% deposit, progress payments, and final payment on completion.

### Common Renovation Types for New Homeowners

**[Kitchen renovation](/en/services/kitchen/) ($15,000–$72,000):** The most impactful upgrade for daily life and resale value. Many new homeowners in Vancouver update their kitchens to suit their cooking style and aesthetic preferences.

**[Bathroom renovation](/en/services/bathroom/) ($12,000–$60,000):** Modern bathrooms with proper waterproofing, updated fixtures, and contemporary finishes add both comfort and value.

**Basement suite conversion ($40,000–$85,000):** A popular investment for new homeowners looking to offset their mortgage with rental income ($1,400–$2,200/month).

**Whole-house update ($23,000–$150,000+):** If you have bought a home that needs updating throughout, a coordinated [whole-house renovation](/en/services/whole-house/) is more efficient and cost-effective than doing one room at a time.

### Tips for New Immigrants

- **Language support:** Look for contractors who have staff who speak your language. Miscommunication during a renovation can be costly. Our team includes Mandarin and Cantonese speakers.
- **Payment protection:** Never pay cash with no receipt. All payments should be documented and tied to completed milestones in the contract.
- **Warranty:** Insist on a written warranty (1–3 years is standard for workmanship). This protects you if issues arise after completion.
- **Neighbourhood research:** Different neighbourhoods have different permit requirements, housing types, and renovation norms. A contractor experienced in your specific area will save you time and avoid surprises.

### Why New Immigrants Choose Reno Stars

We have worked with hundreds of new Canadian homeowners across Metro Vancouver. Our team provides bilingual service (English, Mandarin, Cantonese), transparent written quotes with line-item pricing, and a 3-year workmanship warranty on every project. We handle permits, inspections, and all the paperwork — you focus on choosing the finishes you love.

[View our completed projects →](/en/projects/) | [Get a free quote →](/zh-Hant/contact/)

## Frequently Asked Questions

### What should new immigrants to Vancouver know before hiring a renovation contractor?
British Columbia''s renovation industry differs significantly from most other countries. Key facts for new arrivals: (1) contractors must be licensed with Consumer Protection BC — verify the licence number before signing anything; (2) WorkSafeBC (WCB) coverage is mandatory — unlicensed or uninsured contractors leave you fully liable for any injury on your property; (3) permits are required for plumbing, electrical, structural, and HVAC work — work without permits creates legal and resale problems; (4) everything should be in writing — verbal agreements are unenforceable in BC construction disputes; (5) payment schedules should be milestone-based, never 50%+ upfront.

### How do permits work for home renovation in Metro Vancouver?
Each Metro Vancouver municipality issues its own building permits (City of Vancouver, City of Burnaby, City of Richmond, etc.). Your renovation contractor applies on your behalf, providing drawings and specifications. Permit fees are 1–3% of renovation cost for most residential projects. Permit approval takes 2–8 weeks depending on scope and municipality. All permit-required work is inspected during construction — the contractor schedules inspections and the inspector approves each phase before the next begins. A Certificate of Occupancy (for larger projects) or a sign-off confirms the work meets BC Building Code.

### What are typical renovation costs in Metro Vancouver for new homeowners?
As a benchmark for new homeowners planning renovations: kitchen renovation $20,000–$65,000, bathroom renovation $12,000–$35,000 per bathroom, [basement suite](/en/guides/basement-suite-cost-vancouver/) conversion $65,000–$130,000, whole house cosmetic refresh $25,000–$65,000, and flooring replacement throughout a 2,000 sqft home $12,000–$22,000. Metro Vancouver renovation costs are higher than Canadian averages due to trade labour demand, material costs, and permit requirements. Getting 3 competitive quotes from licensed contractors is the standard approach to validate pricing.

### How should new immigrants negotiate and sign renovation contracts in BC?
A renovation contract in BC should include: complete written scope of work (materials specified with brand, model, and colour where applicable — never accept "contractor''s choice"), clear start and completion dates, a payment schedule tied to construction milestones (not calendar dates), a change order process for out-of-scope items (each change order should be in writing with a price before work begins), and warranty terms (BC minimum: 1 year labour, 2 years materials under Consumer Protection BC). Do not sign without reading the entire contract. Consider having a trusted bilingual friend review it if English is not your first language.

### What resources are available to help new immigrants navigate Vancouver renovation projects?
BC Consumer Protection (consumerprotection.bc.ca) maintains the licensed contractor database and handles complaints against unlicensed or fraudulent contractors. The BC Housing Authority provides guides on renovation permits and contractor requirements. Reno Stars works regularly with new immigrant homeowners throughout Metro Vancouver and offers consultations in Mandarin and Cantonese for clients who prefer to discuss renovation projects in Chinese. Understanding the Canadian renovation process before signing any contract is the single most important step a new homeowner can take.

Related cost guides: [Kitchen Renovation Cost Vancouver 2026](/en/guides/kitchen-renovation-cost-vancouver/) | [Bathroom Renovation Cost Vancouver 2026](/en/guides/bathroom-renovation-cost-vancouver/) | [Whole-House Renovation Cost Vancouver 2026](/en/guides/whole-house-renovation-cost-vancouver/)

