## Frequently Asked Questions

### What are the best pre-sale renovations for a New Westminster home?
Poly-B plumbing replacement delivers outstanding ROI in New Westminster at 150–180%: $6,500–$12,000 cost adds $12,000–$22,000 in value. Full kitchen renovation runs 110–150% ROI ($8,000–$14,000 for countertops + cabinet repaint), while primary bath renovation returns 110–140% ($14,000–$24,000 cost, $20,000–$38,000 value added). Heritage homes in Uptown and Sapperton benefit most from cosmetic updates that preserve original character while modernizing kitchens and bathrooms.

### Does New Westminster have different permit requirements than Burnaby or Surrey?
Yes — New Westminster is an independent city with its own Building Inspection Division (604-527-4580). Heritage overlay zones in Downtown and Uptown have additional design guidelines for exterior changes. Structural work, plumbing relocation, suite creation, and electrical upgrades all require City of New Westminster permits. Most residential permits process in 2–4 weeks.

### What should I fix before listing a New Westminster home?
Commission a pre-listing inspection ($400–$600) first. Key issues in New Westminster''s housing stock: Poly-B plumbing (1978–1995 builds), 100A electrical panels in pre-1995 homes, knob-and-tube wiring in pre-1950 heritage properties, and asbestos in popcorn ceilings of 1970s builds. Older Metro Vancouver homes may also need our [Poly-B Pipe Replacement](/en/services/poly-b-replacement/) service to eliminate unsafe plastic piping. Addressing these before listing prevents buyer negotiation leverage and financing complications.

### Is New Westminster a good market for pre-sale renovation investment?
Yes — New Westminster''s price point ($750K–$1.2M for townhouses and entry detached) creates strong demand from first-time buyers who need move-in-ready properties. The Skytrain corridor (Columbia and Sapperton stations) drives consistent demand. Buyers in this price range are often stretched on financing, making move-in-ready condition a strong differentiator over neighbouring Burnaby or Coquitlam listings.

### What is the ROI on an electrical panel upgrade for pre-sale in New Westminster? Our [Critical Load Panel Installation](/en/services/critical-load-panel/) service upgrades your home electrical capacity for modern renovation demands.

Upgrading from 100A to 200A electrical service in New Westminster costs $3,500–$6,500 and adds $7,000–$14,000 in buyer-perceived value — a return of 150–200%. New Westminster''s pre-1970 heritage homes in Queen''s Park and Downtown frequently have 100A panels that home inspectors flag. A 200A panel removes a common condition and supports modern kitchen appliances, basement suite wiring, and EV charging readiness.

### What is the ROI on LVP flooring for pre-sale in New Westminster?

LVP flooring on the main floor of a New Westminster home costs $4,500–$8,500 and adds $8,000–$16,000 in value — a return of 120–150%. New Westminster pre-war and post-war homes (1920s–1950s) often have original hardwood that is too thin to sand again or has been covered with carpet. LVP over existing subfloor avoids costly hardwood restoration and delivers a clean, modern appearance that photographs well.


