## Frequently Asked Questions

### What is the best flooring for Vancouver homes in 2026?

The best flooring for Metro Vancouver homes depends on location and lifestyle, but the top performers in 2026 are: (1) **Luxury Vinyl Plank (LVP)** — waterproof, durable, 100% basement-safe, $4–$9/sqft installed; best for high-traffic areas and moisture-prone spaces; (2) **Engineered hardwood** — real wood veneer over plywood core, dimensionally stable in Vancouver''s humid climate, $10–$20/sqft installed; (3) **Large-format porcelain tile (24×24 or 12×24)** — most durable, best for kitchens and bathrooms, $8–$20/sqft installed; (4) **Solid hardwood** — premium look and refinishable 5–7 times, $15–$28/sqft installed; NOT recommended for basements or slab-on-grade. For most Metro Vancouver homes, LVP in main living areas + engineered hardwood in bedrooms + large-format tile in bathrooms is the most practical mix.

### Is LVP (luxury vinyl plank) good quality in Vancouver?

Yes — high-quality LVP (6mm+ thick with 12mil+ wear layer) is an excellent choice for Metro Vancouver homes in 2026. Advantages: 100% waterproof (unlike hardwood), pet-scratch resistant, dimensionally stable in humidity fluctuations (important in Vancouver''s climate), comfortable underfoot, and compatible with in-floor heating. Disadvantages: not refinishable, can dent under concentrated weight (furniture legs), and premium LVP still looks like vinyl under close inspection. Top brands available through Metro Vancouver suppliers in 2026: Shaw FloorTe, COREtec, Karndean, and Valinge 5G. Avoid products under 4mm total thickness or 8mil wear layer — they''re disposable-grade.

### Can I install hardwood flooring in a Vancouver basement?

Solid hardwood: never install directly on concrete or in a basement — humidity and moisture will cause cupping, warping, and mould growth. Engineered hardwood: can be installed in above-grade basements with proper moisture testing and a suitable adhesive; glue-down only on concrete (no floating over slab). LVP: the best choice for below-grade basements in Metro Vancouver — 100% waterproof, no acclimation required, approved for direct glue-down or floating over concrete. Reno Stars always does a moisture reading on the slab before specifying any flooring product for a basement project.

### How much does flooring installation cost in Vancouver in 2026?

Flooring installation costs in Metro Vancouver for 2026 (materials + labour, installed price per sqft): LVP $4.50–$9.00, engineered hardwood $10–$20, solid hardwood $15–$28, porcelain tile $8–$22, laminate $4–$8, carpet $3.50–$8. Add 15–20% material waste factor for cuts. A typical 500 sqft main floor in LVP costs $2,250–$4,500 installed; in engineered hardwood $5,000–$10,000. Subfloor prep, floor levelling, and old floor removal add $1–$3/sqft. Reno Stars provides detailed flooring quotes with supply + install pricing for any project over 200 sqft.

### What flooring adds the most value to a Vancouver home?

For resale value in Metro Vancouver, hardwood flooring (solid or engineered) consistently adds the most perceived value — buyers associate it with quality construction. Real estate appraisers in Vancouver value updated hardwood at approximately $5–$10/sqft added value relative to carpet or old vinyl. LVP adds value when replacing dated carpet or vinyl but is perceived as a mid-tier upgrade. For investment properties in Vancouver, LVP is the highest-ROI choice (durable, low maintenance, no refinishing needed). For primary residences targeting the top of the market ($1.5M+), engineered or solid hardwood floors are expected.

### How does Vancouver''s climate affect flooring selection for local homes?

Vancouver''s wet climate — 1,150mm of annual rainfall, combined with humidity from ocean proximity — has a direct impact on flooring selection. LVP (luxury vinyl plank) is the most climate-resilient flooring for Vancouver: 100% waterproof, dimensionally stable in humidity, and unaffected by underfloor moisture. Solid hardwood expands and contracts significantly in Vancouver''s humidity swings — acceptable in above-grade rooms but not recommended for basements or main-floor areas near exterior doors. Engineered hardwood performs better than solid in Vancouver humidity but still requires acclimatization. Tile and porcelain are unaffected by moisture but feel cold in winter without heated floors.

