## Frequently Asked Questions

### What is the average cost of a bathroom renovation in Vancouver?

The average bathroom renovation cost in Vancouver is $18,000–$45,000+, depending on scope and finish level. A cosmetic refresh (new vanity, paint, fixtures) starts around $8,000–$15,000. A standard renovation with new tile and shower runs $18,000–$25,000. A mid-range remodel with custom features costs $25,000–$35,000, and a luxury renovation with curbless shower and premium tile reaches $35,000–$45,000+.

### Do I need a permit for a bathroom renovation in Vancouver?

Yes — most Metro Vancouver municipalities require permits for any plumbing or electrical changes in a bathroom renovation. This includes moving drains, adding outlets, or changing the electrical panel load. Our [Critical Load Panel Installation](/en/services/critical-load-panel/) service upgrades your home electrical capacity for modern renovation demands. Budget $500–$1,500 for permits. Your contractor should handle the permit application. Reno Stars manages all permits as part of the renovation process.

### How long does a bathroom renovation take in Vancouver?

A single bathroom renovation typically takes 2–4 weeks from demo to final inspection. Two bathrooms done simultaneously take 4–5 weeks. Three or more bathrooms run 6–8 weeks. Timeline depends on permit processing (5–15 business days in most Metro Vancouver cities), custom-order lead times for materials, and scope complexity.

### How much does a tub-to-shower conversion cost in Vancouver?

A tub-to-shower conversion in Vancouver typically costs $4,000–$8,000, including demo, waterproofing, tile, and a new shower door. If you keep the drain in its existing location, costs stay toward the lower end. Moving the drain adds $1,500–$3,000. A full walk-in curbless shower with premium tile can reach $10,000–$15,000 as part of a larger renovation.

### Is renovating two bathrooms at once cheaper than doing them separately?

Yes — bundling two or more bathrooms saves 10–15% compared to doing them in separate projects. Labour mobilization costs (project manager time, protection and setup) are shared. If you''re already disrupting one bathroom, doing a second simultaneously is the most cost-efficient approach in Metro Vancouver.

