## Frequently Asked Questions

### How much does bathtub replacement cost in Vancouver?

Bathtub replacement costs in Metro Vancouver range from $800 to $8,500+ depending on the tub type and installation complexity. A standard alcove acrylic tub (supply + installation): $800–$2,200. A freestanding soaker tub supply + installation: $2,500–$6,000. A walk-in tub: $4,000–$10,000. A deep soaker or Japanese-style tub: $3,500–$8,000. Additional costs: retiling the surround after tub swap ($2,000–$6,000), moving the drain ($1,500–$3,500), new faucet and valve ($400–$1,500). A simple like-for-like acrylic tub replacement in the same alcove with a basic surround tile costs $3,500–$7,000 all-in for most Metro Vancouver bathrooms.

### Is it worth replacing a bathtub in Vancouver?

It depends on your goals. For resale value: a primary bathroom with a freestanding soaker tub or a new walk-in shower is highly valued in Metro Vancouver''s market — many buyers specifically request either a soaker tub OR a large walk-in shower (not necessarily both). For homes under $1.5M, a soaker tub adds appeal; over $2M, buyers often prefer a large spa shower with no tub in the primary ensuite. For family homes: a practical alcove tub in the main bathroom is expected — buyers with young children strongly prefer it. Reno Stars always discusses resale intent vs. personal preference before recommending a tub or shower conversion.

### How long does a bathtub replacement take in Vancouver?

A standard alcove bathtub replacement takes 3–5 business days: Day 1 — remove old tub and surround tile; Days 2–3 — install new tub, water connection, backer board, tile; Days 4–5 — tile grouting, caulking, faucet installation, cleanup. If the floor tile needs matching or the plumbing rough-in needs adjusting, add 1–2 days. Freestanding tub installation cost (no surround tile): $3,500–$7,500 (see pricing table above), taking 1–2 days after rough-in plumbing is in place. A bathtub-to-shower conversion (new drain location, tile shower, glass door) takes 5–10 days depending on complexity.

### What is the difference between a soaker tub and a jetted tub in Vancouver?

A soaker tub has no jets — it relies on depth (typically 18–24 inches) for the immersive experience. A jetted tub (whirlpool) has water jets powered by a pump. In Metro Vancouver''s 2026 renovation market, soaker tubs dramatically outsell jetted tubs: soakers are easier to clean, require no maintenance on the pump, and align with the clean, minimalist aesthetic popular in Vancouver. Jetted tubs peaked in the 1990s–2000s and are now associated with dated décor; many homeowners remove jetted tubs during renovations. Reno Stars installs freestanding soakers and alcove soakers regularly; jetted tubs are a special-order item requested by less than 5% of bath clients.

### Can I convert my bathtub to a walk-in shower in Vancouver?

Yes — tub-to-shower conversion is one of the most common bathroom renovation requests in Metro Vancouver. Requirements: moving the drain to the new shower footprint (requires saw-cutting concrete slab in older homes: $1,500–$3,500 for wood-frame buildings it''s simpler); installing a shower pan or building a tile shower; adding a shower valve and head; and installing a glass door or frameless enclosure. Total cost: $6,000–$18,000 depending on whether the drain needs relocation, the tile selected, and the glass door style. Note: if you have a condo or strata, drain relocation requires strata approval and may require a mechanical engineer''s review of the plumbing stack impact. Older Metro Vancouver homes may also need our [Poly-B Pipe Replacement](/en/services/poly-b-replacement/) service to eliminate unsafe plastic piping. See our guide to [BC Strata Renovation Rules](/zh-Hant/blog/strata-renovation-rules-vancouver/) for Metro Vancouver homeowners.

### What bathtub material is best for a Vancouver bathroom?

Bathtub material comparison for Vancouver homes: Acrylic alcove tub: $800–$2,200 installed (2–4 days). Best for rental units, secondary bathrooms, and fast refreshes. Lightweight, easy to install, retains heat poorly. Cast iron or steel alcove: $1,500–$3,500 installed (3–5 days). Best for long-term family homes — excellent heat retention, durable, and refinishable. Heavy (200–400 lbs): confirm your floor structure can handle it. Drop-in tub plus new tile surround: $2,500–$5,500 installed (1–2 weeks). Best for primary bathrooms with a renovation in progress — the surround tile elevates the overall finish level significantly.

